### 💻 Code

<!-- Please post the relevant code sample here as well-->
```ts
interface Descriptor<X> {
  type: X;
}

// Conditional type required as a workaround when filtering properties by type
// https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/issues/38646
type ComposedProperties<T> = T extends any ? { 
  [K in keyof T as T[K] extends Descriptor<any> ? K : never]: 
    T[K] extends Descriptor<infer X> ? X : never;
} : never;

interface Context<T> {
  props: ComposedProperties<T>;
}

type Composer<T> = {
  <F extends (ctx: Context<T>) => any>(fn: F) : Descriptor<ReturnType<F>>;
}

class Composable {
  compose: Composer<this> = undefined!; // Omitted..
}

class Foo extends Composable {
  a = this.compose(() => 'A');
  b = this.compose(() => 2);
  c = 'c';
}

class Bar extends Foo {
  working = this.compose(ctx => ctx.props)  // <-- IF THIS WORKS...

  notWorking = this.compose(ctx => ctx.props.a) // <-- WHY THIS DOESN'T ??
}
```

### 🙁 Actual behavior

'notWorking' implicitly has type 'any' because it does not have a type annotation and is referenced directly or indirectly in its own initializer.(7022)

### 🙂 Expected behavior

No error

<!-- What you expected to happen instead, and why -->

### 🔎 Search Terms

Type guard narrowing never

### 🕗 Version & Regression Information

- This is the behavior in every version I tried

### ⏯ Playground Link

https://www.typescriptlang.org/es/play?ts=5.4.0-beta#

### 💻 Code

```ts

class F {
  isA(): this is A { return this instanceof A }
  isB(): this is B { return this instanceof B }
}

class A extends F {}
class B extends F {}

const aInstance = new A();
const bInstance = new B();

if (aInstance.isA() || aInstance.isB()) console.log("Is A or B"); 
```

### 🙁 Actual behavior

The aInstance.isA() type guard fails, it makes aInstance of type never.

### 🙂 Expected behavior

If aInstance.isA() is true, then aInstance must be of type A 

