Knockout models, which are very concise in javascript, are obnoxious to define in typescript (one of the few things that gets _longer_ in typescript).  I'm suggesting the following syntax to make it much cleaner.  (Perhaps this is a duplicate?)

This syntax adds a constructor signature with parameters which can be referenced in the field initializers.  This is to replace javascript's easy `this.someNewProperty = '...'` legitimacy while retaining the strong typing information of typescript (that is, not resorting to a cast as `any`, etc)

```
class koSubModel(node: any = {}) {
    public a = ko.observable<number>(node.a || 0);
    public b = ko.observable<string>(node.b);
}

class koModel(node: any = {}) {
    public one = ko.observable<number>(node.one || 0);
    public two = ko.observable<string>(node.two || '[missing data]');
    public three = ko.observableArray<number>(node.three || []);
    public four = ko.observableArray<koSubModel>((node.four || []).map(e => new koSubModel(e)));
}

// example usage
$.getJSON('...', data => ko.applyBindings(new koModel(data)));
```

The generated model seems pretty much what you'd write by hand:

```
var koSubModel = (function () {
    function koSubModel(node) {
        if (typeof node === "undefined") { node = {}; }
        this.a = ko.observable(node.a || 0);
        this.b = ko.observable(node.b);
    };
    return koSubModel;
})();

var koModel = (function () {
    function koModel(node) {
        if (typeof node === "undefined") { node = {}; }
        this.one = ko.observable(node.one || 0);
        this.two = ko.observable(node.two || '[missing data]');
        this.three = ko.observableArray((node.three || []));
        this.four = ko.observableArray((node.four || []).map(function (e) { return new koSubModel(e); }));
    };
    return koModel;
})();
```

The alternative (current way) is that all the fields have to be declared and separately assigned in a constructor:

```
class koSubModel_ {
    public a : KnockoutObservable<number>;
    public b : KnockoutObservable<string>;

    constructor(node : any = {}) {
        this.a = ko.observable<number>(node.a || 0);
        this.b = ko.observable<string>(node.b);
    }
}

class koModel_ {
    public one : KnockoutObservable<number>;
    public two : KnockoutObservable<string>;
    public three : KnockoutObservableArray<number>;
    public four : KnockoutObservableArray<koSubModel_>;

    constructor(node: any = {}) {
        this.one = ko.observable<number>(node.one || 0);
        this.two = ko.observable<string>(node.two || '[missing data]');
        this.three = ko.observableArray<number>(node.three || []);
        this.four = ko.observableArray<koSubModel_>((node.four || []).map(e => new koSubModel_(e)));
    }
}
```

Hey TypeScript people! This may just be a documentation issue, but the [release notes](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/release-notes/typescript-3-0.html) for TypeScript 3.0 state the following:

```
The following two declarations are equivalent:
declare function foo(...args: [number, string, boolean]): void;
declare function foo(args_0: number, args_1: string, args_2: boolean): void;
```

However, this does not seem to be true. Using the [TypeScript PlayGround](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play/), the following TypeScript:

```
function foo(...args: [number, string, boolean]): void {
    console.log(args[0]);
}

foo(4, "4", true);
```

Actually compiles to:

```
function foo() {
    var args = [];
    for (var _i = 0; _i < arguments.length; _i++) {
        args[_i] = arguments[_i];
    }
    console.log(args[0]);
}
foo(4, "4", true);
```

So, I think you may need to update your docs! Or am I just missing something obvious here?! Or does your PlayGround need an upgrade?

With the new types such as Pick, Partial etc, could we possibly introduce the idea of a type to Dearray an array? A use case I need it for. I define my DB tables, and use a script to generate an interface an object of field names that matches to their string literal.
From that, I want to specify an array of allowed updatable fields, then create a sub interface from that array using Pick.

```ts
var UserFields = {
    firstName: <"firstName">"firstName",
    lastName: <"lastName">"lastName",
    password: <"password">"password"
}; //This is generated by script

interface IUserFields {
    firstName: string;
    lastName: string;
    password: string;
}; //This is generated by script

var changeableFields = [UserFields.firstName, UserFields.lastName] //Type is ("firstName" | "lastName")[];

//Now I want to de-arrayify this type so I can use it in a Pick
//Something like this
type IUpdateableFields = Pick<IUserFields, Dearray<typeof changeableFields>>; //Type is {firstName: string; lastName: string}
```

Given this code:

``` typescript
interface A {
    a?: B;
}

interface B {
    a: string;
    b: string;
}

let a: A;
console.log(a.a.b);
```

I want it to error on the line:

``` typescript
console.log(a.a.b);
```

because I didn't add a check:

``` typescript
if(a.a) {
    console.log(a.a.b);
}
```

I think access undefined errors are one of the most common bugs.

Current implementaiton:

``` ts
interface Promise<T> {
    then<TResult>(onfulfilled?: (value: T) => TResult | PromiseLike<TResult>, onrejected?: (reason: any) => TResult | PromiseLike<TResult>): Promise<TResult>;
    then<TResult>(onfulfilled?: (value: T) => TResult | PromiseLike<TResult>, onrejected?: (reason: any) => void): Promise<TResult>;

    catch(onrejected?: (reason: any) => T | PromiseLike<T>): Promise<T>;
}
```

What I believe is a more correct implementation:

``` ts
interface Promise<T> {
    then<TResult>(onfulfilled?: (value: T) => TResult | PromiseLike<TResult>, onrejected?: (reason: any) => TResult | PromiseLike<TResult>): Promise<TResult>;
    then<TResult>(onfulfilled?: (value: T) => TResult | PromiseLike<TResult>, onrejected?: (reason: any) => void): Promise<TResult>;

    catch<TResult>(onrejected?: (reason: any) => TResult | PromiseLike<TResult>): Promise<TResult>;
    catch<TResult>(onrejected?: (reason: any) => void): Promise<TResult>;
}
```

I believe the spec says that `catch(x)` behaves the same as `then(undefined, x)`, which implies that catch should be a generic method with a `TResult` type.

If I am misunderstanding the spec, enlightenment would be appreciated.

Shorthand initializer with kind of 'nameof' for the expression:
```ts
var a = {x: 1, y: 2, z: 3};
// Shorthand form
var b = {a.x, a.y};
// Long form
var b = {x: a.x, y: a.y};
```

Are there plans for this?
Couldn't find similar suggestion.

Would it be possible for the compiler to 'retype' or subtype variables?
I ask because I find myself using a lot of overloaded function parameters, and during the function it would be nice to be able to do something like the following:

``` Typescript
class TypeA {
    a(): string;
}

class TypeB {
    b(): number;
}
...
function (objecta: TypeA);
function (objectb: TypeB);
function (object: any) {
    if (object instanceof TypeA) {
        //treat object as a type of A.
        object.b(); //<-- this should error!
    }
}
```

Now, it would be _nice_ if typescript could look at that expression, and determine that object is an instanceof TypeA, and then treat it as TypeA. However this might be a little hard, so another option would be for a developer to add a keyword, or a line for the TS compiler to tell it that the variable's type has changed:
e.g.

``` Typescript
    if (object instanceof TypeA) {
        now object: TypeA;
        object.b(); //<-- this should error!
    }
```

(Keyword can be changed to something more appropriate).

And the Typescript compiler would only apply that typing for the current scope (this would get compiled out of JS code).

I know I can just assign the object to another variable, but I find it starts making the code less readable.

