**Describe the bug**

`redis-cli` appears to leak a reply object in the legacy help initialization path when a server returns an unexpected `COMMAND` reply structure.

In `cliLegacyIntegrateHelp()` (`src/redis-cli.c`), `redis-cli` calls `COMMAND` and iterates over the returned entries. If the top-level reply is an array but one of its elements has an unexpected type/shape, the function returns early without freeing the original `reply`.

This seems to be a small robustness issue rather than a high-impact security problem, but it would be nice to clean it up for better protocol error handling.

Relevant code snippet:

```c
static void cliLegacyIntegrateHelp(void) {
    if (cliConnect(CC_QUIET) == REDIS_ERR) return;

    redisReply *reply = redisCommand(context, "COMMAND");
    if (reply == NULL) return;
    if (reply->type != REDIS_REPLY_ARRAY) {
        freeReplyObject(reply);
        return;
    }

    for (size_t j = 0; j < reply->elements; j++) {
        redisReply *entry = reply->element[j];
        if (entry->type != REDIS_REPLY_ARRAY || entry->elements < 4 ||
            entry->element[0]->type != REDIS_REPLY_STRING ||
            entry->element[1]->type != REDIS_REPLY_INTEGER ||
            entry->element[3]->type != REDIS_REPLY_INTEGER) return;
        ...
    }

    freeReplyObject(reply);
}
```

If one `entry` inside the `COMMAND` reply has an unexpected type/shape, the function returns early from inside the loop, and the original `reply` is not freed.

